---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const facts = [
  ["Day job", "AI and software engineer at Proshore, building AI assistants that read a company's documents so people don't have to."],
  ["Phone calls answered", "90,000+. Not by me, by a Nepali voice bot I built. It never got tired; I did."],
  ["Proudest tiny model", "A parasite-egg detector that lost 58% of its weight and got more useful. Peer-reviewed, even."],
  ["Lowest-level flex", "A chat server in plain C. Sockets, threads, no frameworks, a lot of valgrind."],
  ["Most fun side quest", "Minecraft bots that pick a leader, build castles together and rescue each other at night."],
  ["When not coding", "Making music, designing posters for developer events, and building a piano for the browser."],
];
---
<Base title="About" description="Gaurav Giri: AI engineer in Kathmandu who teaches computers to listen, talk and see, mostly in Nepali.">
  <h1 class="display text-5xl">Hi, I'm Gaurav.</h1>
  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <p class="lead">I teach computers to listen, talk and look at things, preferably in Nepali. I'm an AI engineer in Kathmandu, a licensed computer engineer, and the person whose 3D face is on the <a href="/">home page</a> (go on, say hi to it).</p>
    <blockquote><p>Explorer of knowledge, not prisoner of perfection.</p></blockquote>
  </div>

  <h2 class="title mt-12 text-2xl">The quick version</h2>
  <dl class="mt-4 divide-y divide-haze border-y border-haze">
    {facts.map(([k, v]) => (
      <div class="grid gap-1 py-4 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-6">
        <dt class="font-semibold">{k}</dt>
        <dd class="text-slate">{v}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <div class="prose mt-12">
    <h2>How I got here</h2>
    <p><strong>High school:</strong> I learned Python to break into things (legally). Nights went to TryHackMe rooms, mornings to pretending I'd slept.</p>
    <p><strong>The pandemic:</strong> stuck at home, I collected languages like souvenirs: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP, C and C++. Then I met machine learning, and I never really left.</p>
    <p><strong>College:</strong> Computer Engineering at Kantipur Engineering College, finished with distinction. Somewhere between the labs I won a few hackathons (one for a maths game starring a witch), placed 7th in Nepal at IEEEXtreme, and published two papers: one on spotting parasite eggs under a microscope, one on recognising birds by their songs.</p>
    <p><strong>Work:</strong> five years of freelance apps and backends, including a Flutter app for the Bagmati Provincial Assembly. Then a year making machines speak Nepali at ekGhanti: speech recognition, a cloned voice, and a phone bot that took those 90,000 calls. Now I build AI agents at Proshore.</p>

    <h2>Things I care about</h2>
    <p><strong>Nepali AI.</strong> Most AI is built for English first. I like making it work for the language I think in.</p>
    <p><strong>Small, honest models.</strong> If it runs on a cheap phone and admits when it doesn't know, I'm happy.</p>
    <p><strong>Community.</strong> I'm the Ed-Tech Lead at Google Developer Groups Kathmandu, where I help run DevFest and Google I/O Extended. I've also organised UbuCon Asia 2025, taught Git to 50+ students as a Leapfrog Student Partner, and I'm a Notion Campus Leader, which means I have strong opinions about how you organise your notes.</p>

    <h2>Say hi</h2>
    <p>Talking about AI, Nepali speech tech, music or a role you're hiring for? Email <a href="mailto:gaurovgiri@gmail.com">gaurovgiri@gmail.com</a> or find me on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/thegauravgiri">LinkedIn</a>. Or skip the inbox and ask my avatar on the <a href="/">home page</a>; it knows my CV better than I do.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
